    <string name="saver_title">Network guard</string>
    <string name="saver_subtitle">Keep the apps you choose off the internet — locally, without root.</string>
    <string name="saver_consent_title">Step one: VPN consent</string>
    <string name="saver_consent_body">Android requires explicit consent before any app can create a VPN tunnel. The system dialog appears once; after that, Mizan can block traffic from the apps you block on your device.</string>
    <string name="saver_consent_action">Grant VPN consent</string>
    <string name="saver_consent_granted_title">Consent granted</string>
    <string name="saver_consent_granted_body">Protection does not start automatically.</string>
    <string name="saver_how_title">How blocking works</string>
    <string name="saver_how_body">When enabled, Android routes only the blocked apps into a local tunnel inside Mizan. Their packets never leave the device; they are dropped here. Every other app stays outside the tunnel and works normally.</string>
    <string name="saver_privacy_title">Private by design</string>
    <string name="saver_privacy_body">Mizan has no internet permission, so it cannot send your data to any server. No analytics, no accounts, and every list and decision stays on your device.</string>
    <string name="saver_limits_title">Limits to know</string>
    <string name="saver_limits_body">One VPN at a time: enabling Mizan\u2019s guard disconnects any other VPN. A VPN icon appears in the status bar. Blocked apps are blocked on metered networks only (usually mobile); on unmetered Wi-Fi they work normally.</string>
    <string name="saver_revoke_note">You can revoke consent from the system\u2019s VPN settings at any time.</string>
